 //! A Wayland window presents Exact's painted frames through a compositor
 //! surface. Winit waits for compositor input, keyboard repeats and presenter work.
+//!
+//! An app with a status button that the desktop's tray takes (`tray`) shows
+//! no window at its launch: the window is made when the item is clicked and
+//! destroyed when it is put away (Wayland has no hidden toplevel), while
+//! the presenter, and the page, run on.
+//!
+//! The tray's window hangs below the item, as the Mac's popover does. A
+//! Wayland client cannot place a toplevel (the compositor does, and GNOME
+//! centres it), so an app the tray takes runs its window through XWayland
+//! when there is one, and opens it at the point the tray says was clicked.
+//! The page boots before the event loop is made, so that choice is known.
